<section id="Certificate-of-Proficiency" style="" class="dccc-accordion-block style_1">
  <div class="container">
          <h2 class="dccc-accordion-block__title title_h2 col-12">FAQs</h2>
        <div class="dccc-accordion-block__list col-12">
                <div class="dccc-accordion-block__item dccc-accordion-block-item">
            <div class="dccc-accordion-block-item__title">
                              <button
                  type="button"
                  class="acc-item-title dccc-accordion-block-item__trigger"
                  id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-1"
                  aria-expanded="false"
                  aria-controls="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-1"
                >
                  <span class="acc-item-title__text">What is a “Sponsoring” school district?</span>
                </button>
                          </div>
                          <div
                class="dccc-accordion-block-item__content"
                id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-1"
                role="region"
                aria-labelledby="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-1"
                aria-hidden="true"
                tabindex="-1"
                hidden
              >
                                                      <p>A sponsoring school district is a school district in Delaware County that<br />
contributes financially to the Delaware County Community College budget and<br />
provides reduced admission to the College for residents of these areas.<br />
Sponsoring school districts in Delaware County are:</p>
<ul>
<li>Chester-Upland</li>
<li>Garnet Valley (Bethel residents only)</li>
<li>Haverford</li>
<li>Interboro</li>
<li>Radnor</li>
<li>Ridley</li>
<li>Rose Tree Media</li>
<li>Southeast Delco</li>
<li>Springfield</li>
<li>Upper Darby</li>
<li>Wallingford-Swarthmore (Swarthmore and Rutledge residents<br />
only)</li>
<li>William Penn</li>
</ul>
<p>There are NO sponsoring school districts in Chester County.<br />
For more information, click on <strong><a title="Tuition &amp; Fees" href="/admissions-financial-aid/cost/tuition-fees">Tuition and Fees</a></strong>.</p>

                                                </div>
                      </div>
                    <div class="dccc-accordion-block__item dccc-accordion-block-item">
            <div class="dccc-accordion-block-item__title">
                              <button
                  type="button"
                  class="acc-item-title dccc-accordion-block-item__trigger"
                  id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-2"
                  aria-expanded="false"
                  aria-controls="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-2"
                >
                  <span class="acc-item-title__text">Who qualifies for &quot;Sponsoring&quot; school district reduced tuition rates?</span>
                </button>
                          </div>
                          <div
                class="dccc-accordion-block-item__content"
                id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-2"
                role="region"
                aria-labelledby="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-2"
                aria-hidden="true"
                tabindex="-1"
                hidden
              >
                                                      <p>Any resident of the following &#8220;Sponsoring&#8221; school districts will benefit from the reduced tuition rates:</p>
<ul>
<li>Chester-Upland</li>
<li>Garnet Valley (Bethel residents only)</li>
<li>Haverford</li>
<li>Interboro</li>
<li>Radnor</li>
<li>Ridley</li>
<li>Rose Tree Media</li>
<li>Southeast Delco</li>
<li>Springfield</li>
<li>Upper Darby</li>
<li>Wallingford-Swarthmore (Swarthmore and Rutledge residents only)</li>
<li>William Penn</li>
</ul>
<p>There are NO sponsoring school districts in Chester County.<br />
For more information, click on <strong><a title="Tuition &amp; Fees" href="/admissions-financial-aid/cost/tuition-fees">Tuition and Fees</a></strong>.</p>

                                                </div>
                      </div>
                    <div class="dccc-accordion-block__item dccc-accordion-block-item">
            <div class="dccc-accordion-block-item__title">
                              <button
                  type="button"
                  class="acc-item-title dccc-accordion-block-item__trigger"
                  id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-3"
                  aria-expanded="false"
                  aria-controls="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-3"
                >
                  <span class="acc-item-title__text">How many credit hours do I need to receive an associate degree?</span>
                </button>
                          </div>
                          <div
                class="dccc-accordion-block-item__content"
                id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-3"
                role="region"
                aria-labelledby="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-3"
                aria-hidden="true"
                tabindex="-1"
                hidden
              >
                                                      <p>60-80 credit hours depending on your major. For more information, explore our <strong><a href="https://www.dccc.edu/academics/program-search/">Program Search</a> </strong>tool.</p>

                                                </div>
                      </div>
                    <div class="dccc-accordion-block__item dccc-accordion-block-item">
            <div class="dccc-accordion-block-item__title">
                              <button
                  type="button"
                  class="acc-item-title dccc-accordion-block-item__trigger"
                  id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-4"
                  aria-expanded="false"
                  aria-controls="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-4"
                >
                  <span class="acc-item-title__text">What is a Certificate of Proficiency or Competency?</span>
                </button>
                          </div>
                          <div
                class="dccc-accordion-block-item__content"
                id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-4"
                role="region"
                aria-labelledby="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-4"
                aria-hidden="true"
                tabindex="-1"
                hidden
              >
                                                      <p>A Certificate of Proficiency or Competency is a course of study that provides an overall certificate from the College that generally leads to a new career or enhances a person’s skill set to make them more productive and marketable.</p>
<p>For a Certificate of Proficiency, students must complete 30 credits of an approved career program and will usually consist of 24 credits in the career specialty and six credits in general education. At least 50 percent of the credits must be earned at Delaware County Community College.</p>
<p>Students must have a GPA of 2.0 or higher and at least six credit hours must be in courses that are awarded grade points.</p>
<p>For a Certificate of Competency, students must complete an approved credit-bearing career program that requires less than 30 credits. The student must have a cumulative GPA of 2.0 or higher. At least six credit hours must be in courses that are awarded grade points.</p>
<p>For credit bearing Certificates of Proficiency or Competency, explore our <a href="https://www.dccc.edu/academics/program-search/"><strong>Program Search</strong></a> tool or call the Admissions Office at 610-359-5050. For non-credit bearing Certification, visit the <a href="https://www.dccc.edu/continuing-education/"><strong>Continuing Education</strong></a> web page or call 610-359-5362.</p>

                                                </div>
                      </div>
                    <div class="dccc-accordion-block__item dccc-accordion-block-item">
            <div class="dccc-accordion-block-item__title">
                              <button
                  type="button"
                  class="acc-item-title dccc-accordion-block-item__trigger"
                  id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-5"
                  aria-expanded="false"
                  aria-controls="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-5"
                >
                  <span class="acc-item-title__text">How many classes or credits do I need to take to receive Financial Aid?</span>
                </button>
                          </div>
                          <div
                class="dccc-accordion-block-item__content"
                id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-5"
                role="region"
                aria-labelledby="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-5"
                aria-hidden="true"
                tabindex="-1"
                hidden
              >
                                                      <p>If students are offered a Federal Pell Grant, they must register for at least one credit per term to receive a portion of the Pell Grant. The amount of grant increases with the number of credits (3 credits 25 percent, 6 credits 50 percent, 9 credits 75 percent, and 12 plus credits 100 percent).</p>
<p>If students are offered a Federal Direct Loan, they must register for at least six credits per term to receive any loan money.</p>
<p>Federal financial aid will not cover more than 30 developmental or ESL credits, and not more than 90 credits total at the college. For more information, click on <a href="https://www.dccc.edu/admissions-and-financial-aid/financial-aid/"><strong>Financial Aid</strong></a>.</p>

                                                </div>
                      </div>
                    <div class="dccc-accordion-block__item dccc-accordion-block-item">
            <div class="dccc-accordion-block-item__title">
                              <button
                  type="button"
                  class="acc-item-title dccc-accordion-block-item__trigger"
                  id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-6"
                  aria-expanded="false"
                  aria-controls="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-6"
                >
                  <span class="acc-item-title__text">How do I withdraw from the College for Medical Reasons or Military  Service?</span>
                </button>
                          </div>
                          <div
                class="dccc-accordion-block-item__content"
                id="Certificate-of-Proficiency-panel-6"
                role="region"
                aria-labelledby="Certificate-of-Proficiency-trigger-6"
                aria-hidden="true"
                tabindex="-1"
                hidden
              >
                                                      <p>The College recognizes that students may have to officially withdraw from class(es) because of a call to active military duty (appropriately certified), a disabling injury or serious illness (must be certified by a physician), a life-changing event over which they have no control (must be documented by an appropriate third party), or an administrative reason (must have a staff member provide written explanation to support the request). Upon receipt of the withdrawal form and documentation, the request will be reviewed and may take 4-6 weeks for a decision. The request may be made within the term or no later than 30 days following the term.</p>
<p><strong>NOTE:</strong> Future financial aid eligibility may be adversely affected by withdrawals. All students requesting withdrawals are encouraged to speak with the Financial Aid and Student Accounts Offices prior to submitting the form and supporting documentation to determine the potential financial implications based on a possible change in enrollment.</p>
<p>Please be prepared to attach the appropriate supporting documentation when submitting the request form.</p>
<p>Current students should log into delaGATE and complete the Administrative Withdrawal form in Students/Registration and Payment/Withdrawal &amp; Refund Info section. If you have any questions about this process, please email <a href="mailto:records@dccc.edu"><strong>records@dccc.edu</strong></a>.</p>

                                                </div>
                      </div>
              </div>
  </div>
</section>


